The '''Decline and Recovery period (1905–1917)''' covers the extended economic downturn that followed the [[Second War of the Isthmus]] and the Mild Depression, the political fragmentation of an expanded [[Asamblea Nacional]], the rise of anti-Volisanian sentiment, and the eventual pivot toward tourism as a new economic base. While the broader regional economy recovered from the Mild Depression by 1908, Guajicutea's interconnected economy continued to decline through 1911. A moderate coalition government elected in 1912 established the [[Línea Guajicuteana]] cruise line in 1913, and the economy began to recover in 1915–1916, supported by a loan from [[Sarta]].

== Extended economic decline ==

The Mild Depression that began in 1901 ended for most of the region by 1908, but Guajicutea's economy continued to contract through 1911. The island's economy was deeply integrated into regional trade networks through exports, imported raw materials, and foreign-owned industry, making it disproportionately vulnerable to external shocks. When the regional economy contracted, demand for Guajicutean exports collapsed further, and the recovery that benefited less integrated economies passed Guajicutea by.

The shutdown of the Volisanian-owned ironworks in [[Waribá]] in 1905 was the single most significant industrial event of the period. The ironworks had been the island's principal heavy industrial installation; its closure threw hundreds of workers out of employment in a city already suffering from the depression. Unemployment, which had been 5% from 1885 through 1900, jumped to 11% in 1905 and peaked at approximately 19% around 1908 before declining to 15% by 1910 as workers left the industrial sector for subsistence agriculture.

The decline produced violent protests against the state, escalating from the labour disputes and police clashes of the previous era into broader anti-state violence. Targets included government buildings, police stations, and foreign-owned businesses. The [[Noche de las Camisas Rojas]] in 1905, in which communist-backed forces inspired by the [[Darasian Socialist Revolution]] attempted to seize a police station in Waribá, marked the first armed confrontation between organised political radicals and state security forces in Guajicutean history and foreshadowed the street-level conflicts of the following years.

== Movimiento de la Yaya ==

From 1905, an anti-industrialization movement gained momentum across Guajicutea. The [[Movimiento de la Yaya]], named from ''yaya'' (earth or ground in the Guaji language), advocated a return to traditional agricultural life. The movement drew support from both ends of the political spectrum for different reasons. The left objected to industrialization as worker exploitation and foreign ownership of labour; the right objected to industrialization as foreign influence and the loss of traditional Guaji culture. Both converged on the same prescription: a return to the cultivation of cassava, yams, and makú, the three staple crops of pre-colonial Guajicutea.

The movement was connected to the [[Bikuismo|Bikuismo]] cultural revival. "Return to the earth" was both an economic programme and a cultural statement, drawing on the same pre-colonial heritage that artists were incorporating into contemporary works after the rediscovery of the catacombs in 1892. The Movimiento de la Yaya was not a political party but a cross-party movement; both [[Partido Socialista de Guaji|PSG]] (socialist) and [[Nuevo Partido de Aislamiento|NPdA]] (fascist) elements participated, though for fundamentally different reasons.

The movement's effect on employment data was visible in the 1910 census. The agricultural share of the workforce, which had declined from 65% in 1885 to 48% in 1905, rebounded to 55% by 1910. This shift partly reflected genuine ideological commitment and partly economic necessity: with industrial employment contracting and unemployment high, subsistence agriculture was a survival strategy as much as a political statement.

== Political fragmentation ==

The [[Asamblea Nacional]], expanded from 32 to 150 seats in 1900, was marked by extreme division and periodic physical violence. Six parties held seats: the [[Partido del Internacionalismo|PdI]] (free trade), [[Partido por la Tierra|PpLT]] (rural labour), [[Partido de Estabilización|PpE]] (nativist labour), [[Partido de los Trabajadores|PdT]] (urban multi-racial workers), [[Partido Socialista de Guaji|PSG]] (socialist), and [[Nuevo Partido de Aislamiento|NPdA]] (fascist). No single party or natural coalition could command a governing majority. Physical fights broke out in the assembly between members, parliamentary business was frequently disrupted, and governance was difficult with this level of fragmentation.

President [[Álvaro Prieto]] (PdI, 1905–1909) presided over this period as a weak executive unable to build a stable governing coalition. The depression deepened, protests intensified, and the assembly's dysfunction prevented coordinated policy responses.

== Anti-Volisanian sentiment ==

Throughout 1905–1911, anti-Volisanian sentiment grew across the political spectrum. [[Volisania]] was blamed for losing the [[Second War of the Isthmus]] (1893–1899), for the ironworks closure that threw hundreds out of work, and for the foreign ownership of private institutions that controlled significant parts of Guajicutea's economy. The sentiment cut across party lines: both the PSG (socialist) and the NPdA (fascist) exploited it, though from different ideological positions.

== Right-wing conservative coalition (1910) ==

In 1910, a right-wing conservative coalition of the PpE and PdI took power under President [[Gerardo Santos]] (PpE, 1909–1913). The coalition was conservative, not fascist: the NPdA supported its anti-Volisanian measures from outside the coalition, but the government drew a line between conservative protectionism and fascist radicalism.

The coalition enacted two anti-Volisanian measures. The first banned Volisanian ownership of private industries in Guajicutea, requiring Volisanian companies to divest their Guajicutean assets. The second banned the Volisanian language in commercial goods: products sold in Guajicutea were required to have labeling, packaging, and commercial text written in Sartan, not Volisanian.

The coalition welcomed [[Sarta|Sartan]] investment to replace Volisanian capital. Sarta, the former colonial power, was seen as friendly, and Sartan investment was invited to fill the gap left by the divestment of Volisanian companies. The Sartan government, however, recommended moderation: Sarta maintained friendly relations with Volisania and advised Guajicutea against pursuing overly harsh measures. The Sartan government served as a moderating influence on the coalition's policies.

== Trade diversification (1910–1912) ==

With Volisanian capital being divested and the industrial base weakened, Guajicutea sought to diversify its trade relationships. Increased dealings with [[Zong]]-controlled [[Balboa]] and with [[Balisca]] opened new commercial channels. However, Guajicutea's industrial base was weak: the ironworks was gone, manufacturing had contracted, and most of the workforce was employed in reprocessing — the light processing of agricultural products to add value to primary goods.

Guajicutea's exports during this period consisted of reprocessed agricultural products (cassava flour, dried fish, tobacco processing), guano from [[Warikú]], diminished gold from the southern highlands, and traditional crafts — pottery, textiles, and carving connected to the Bikuismo cultural revival, as pre-colonial art forms entered commercial production.

== Moderate coalition (1912) ==

In 1912, a moderate coalition was elected, composed of the PpLT, PdT, and the moderate wing of the PdI. The coalition rejected both PSG socialist radicalism and NPdA fascist radicalism, offering a centrist response to the crisis. President [[Raúl Campos]] (PpLT, 1913–1917) led the government.

The moderate coalition brought a measure of stability after the chaos of 1905–1911. Its programme focused on economic diversification and the development of tourism as an alternative to industrial production.

== Línea Guajicuteana (1913) ==

In 1913, the Campos government devised a cruise line scheme, establishing the '''Línea Guajicuteana''' — a government-funded cruise line positioning Guajicutea as a stop on the route from [[Illypnia]] and [[Kaftia]] to [[Abyala]], primarily [[Tapuya]] and [[Balboa]]. The scheme exploited Guajicutea's geographic position in the [[Capuyaquiran Sea]] as an asset, targeting Illypnian and Kaftian tourists traveling to Abyala.

The scheme required public investment in tourism infrastructure: ports, guest facilities, and cruise vessels. Tourism represented a new economic sector for Guajicutea, an alternative to the industrial production that had failed during the depression.

== Economic recovery (1915–1916) ==

The pivot measures began to produce results in 1915–1916. Tourism revenue started flowing as cruise lines became operational, and trade diversification with Zong Balboa and Balisca matured. Guajicutea secured a loan from [[Sarta]] to fund the recovery and pivot infrastructure. Sarta, the former colonial power, provided the loan as part of the investment relationship that had been encouraged since the 1910 right-wing coalition welcomed Sartan capital.

The recovery was gradual rather than a boom. The extended decline that had persisted since 1905 came to an end, and by 1917 the economy was stabilizing, though still fragile. The industrial base remained weak, and the economy's dependence on tourism and reprocessed agricultural goods represented a structural shift from the industrial aspirations of the previous era.

== Demographics ==

The population of Guajicutea grew from approximately 340,000 in 1905 to approximately 370,000 by 1917, a growth rate of approximately 0.7% per year, slower than the previous era due to the extended economic depression.

